  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>The reaction is known as hydroboration-oxidation. In this reaction, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>6</mn></msub></math> adds to the double bond of propene, resulting in the formation of an organoborane intermediate. Subsequent treatment with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> converts the organoborane intermediate to an alcohol. Hydration follows anti-Markownikoff's rule.</p><p>In this hydration reaction, 1-methylcyclohexene (an alkene) is reacted with aqueous <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> (formed from water and a strong acid such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math>), resulting in Markovnikov addition of water across the pi bond. The product is an alcohol.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/6bd88670-0c2c-4dcc-b3ef-338d5549b889.png\" style=\"width: 547px; height: 375px;\"/></p></blockquote>",
    "image": null
  },
